title: '[pallet-revive] `System.terminate` deletes the contract despite locks, freezes and holds'

doc:
- audience: Runtime User
  description: |-
    `System.terminate` now always deletes the calling contract when it returns success. Before,
    a lock, a freeze or a foreign hold on the contract account, for example from a vested
    transfer to the contract, made the destruction at the end of the call stack fail without an
    error: the call returned success and the contract stayed alive.

    The storage deposit is now refunded to the origin when `System.terminate` is called, before
    the balance is paid out to the beneficiary, instead of at the end of the call stack. If that
    refund fails, the call to `System.terminate` now fails. Before, it returned success. In both
    cases the contract stays alive. Only the part of the deposit that a freeze on the account
    needs stays on hold. That part stays on the leftover account and the origin does not get it
    back. If a contract is later deployed to the same address, whoever terminates that contract
    receives it. The payout sends the transferable balance as before.

    If the account cannot be reaped because something still pins funds on it, the contract is
    deleted and the account stays behind as a plain account holding those funds. A contract
    deployed to the same address later takes the account over. Funds sent to the contract after
    `System.terminate` are forwarded to the beneficiary at the end of the call stack, before the
    ED is burned. This only holds as far as the balance covers the locks and freezes on the
    account: under a lock or freeze larger than the balance only the part below one native unit
    is forwarded and the rest of the late funds stays on the account. If the transfer fails, the
    funds stay on the account as well, and the contract is deleted anyway. The ED is only burned
    when the account holds nothing else afterwards, not even a part of a balance below one native
    unit. Otherwise the ED stays on the account together with the rest, so `BALANCE` of the
    address does not change. Under an encumbrance the ED stays on the account as well. A provider
    or sufficient reference from another pallet, for example a sufficient asset the contract
    holds, does not keep the ED: it is burned and the account stays alive on that reference.

    A contract that names itself as the beneficiary, of `System.terminate` or of a `SELFDESTRUCT`
    in the transaction that created it, is deleted and its balance stays on the account, as in
    EIP-8246. This includes funds that arrive after the call. With a zero balance the ED is burned
    and the account is reaped. Otherwise the ED stays on the account, so `BALANCE` of the address
    keeps returning what the contract held, plus the funds that arrived after the call, including
    the part below one native unit. Before, the ED was burned, which lowered that balance by the
    ED or removed a balance below the ED as dust, and the part below one native unit was dropped.
    On its own, a `SELFDESTRUCT` of a pre-existing contract that names itself still changes no
    balance. A contract that is terminated more than once in a transaction, for example by
    `System.terminate` and a `SELFDESTRUCT`, sends the funds that arrive afterwards to the
    beneficiary it named last.

    A `SELFDESTRUCT` run after `System.terminate`, in the same frame or in a later one, no longer
    cancels the termination: the contract is deleted and its deposit is refunded once.

    `SELFDESTRUCT` keeps its EIP-6780 semantics. A contract created in the same transaction is
    now deleted even if its account carries a lock, which leaves the locked funds on the account.
    This now also holds for `SELFDESTRUCT` in the constructor of a top-level instantiation, which
    before left the contract alive. A `SELFDESTRUCT` in a nested EVM constructor (`new X()`) also
    left the contract alive, because the teardown aborted on the init code hash. When an EVM
    constructor runs `SELFDESTRUCT`, the contract is now deleted and the runtime code it deployed
    and its deposit are released. A pre-existing contract keeps its code and deposits and only
    has its balance moved.

    `ISystem.sol` no longer lists balance locks as a reason for `terminate` to revert. It never
    reverted for that reason.

- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    No migration or configuration change is needed.

    `seal_terminate_logic` still measures one storage deposit refund and the teardown. The
    refund now runs when `System.terminate` is called instead of at the end of the call stack,
    and each teardown step runs in its own storage layer. The `pallet_revive` weights are not
    regenerated for this release and no benchmark was run on it. The existing
    `seal_terminate_logic` weight is 222.45M ps ref_time, 6713 bytes proof size and 7 storage
    reads. The measurement after this change on master is 235.88M ps, 6348 bytes and 6 reads.
    The ref_time here is about 6% below that measurement, while the proof size and reads are
    above it.

    `Deposit::refund_all` now releases the native storage deposit only as far as the freezes on
    the account allow and returns the amount actually released, instead of failing. The PGAS
    backend delegates its native part to the same code, so this applies to both backends.
    `Deposit` is sealed, so no downstream implementation is affected.

    `pallet-revive-fixtures` gains the `TerminateCombined` fixture, which adds the generated
    `TerminateCombined` and `TerminateCombinedCaller` modules. The existing `Terminate` and
    `TerminateCaller` fixtures are unchanged.

crates:
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-revive-uapi
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-revive-fixtures
  bump: minor
